NAME
    set - show or change the shell's own settings

SYNOPSIS
    set [-a] [-e] [-o NAME] [-u] [+a] [+e] [+o NAME] [+u]
    set -- [ARG...]
    set

DESCRIPTION
    Changes the settings the shell keeps for the session. A leading -
    turns a setting on and a leading + turns it off; the two are not
    two spellings of the same thing, and for -a the difference shows.

    -a          every variable is exported from now on, so each later
                assignment exports too (see export). +a stops doing
                that without un-exporting what is already exported: a
                variable's exported state is its own, and +a says only
                `stop doing it from here on'.
    -e          leave the shell at once if a command fails. +e ignores
                failures and carries on.
    -u          referring to a variable that is not set is an error.
                +u treats it as empty.
    -o NAME     the long form of the above, with two more:
                    errexit, nounset, allexport, pipefail
                Each has a +o NAME form that turns it off. A NAME
                this shell does not have is refused, rather than
                accepted and ignored.

    A bare -o with no NAME writes what the shell is currently set to,
    one per line, which is the four settings above:

        allexport  off
        errexit    off
        nounset    off
        pipefail   off

    With --, or with arguments that are not options, the arguments
    become the positional parameters $1, $2, ... of the shell.

    With no other argument, writes every variable and its value, one
    per line:

        NAME=VALUE

    An unknown option is refused with status 2, whether it is spelled
    as a letter or as a -o NAME. Shell builtin.
